WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.502
This briefing comes from Book 08 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook, DNS,

2
00:00:05.502 --> 00:00:10.348
DHCP and IPAM: governing the name
and address layer as one control

3
00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:14.675
plane across the data center,
every cloud and the branch edge.

4
00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:18.614
I'll take four questions: why
DNS is now the control plane,

5
00:00:18.614 --> 00:00:22.638
what has to change, who owns it,
and how to migrate and run it.

6
00:00:22.638 --> 00:00:25.861
The short version: every
connection starts with a name,

7
00:00:25.861 --> 00:00:29.650
so whoever governs the name
layer governs the enterprise.

8
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.925
Why is DNS now the control plane?

9
00:00:32.925 --> 00:00:39.763
OMB said it back in 2008: almost every
network connection begins with a DNS query.

10
00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:42.362
What has changed is how
much the answer decides.

11
00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:48.774
In a hybrid multi-cloud agency, DNS does
six jobs its designers never anticipated.

12
00:00:48.774 --> 00:00:52.405
It publishes private endpoints:
one storage name answers

13
00:00:52.405 --> 00:00:56.187
privately inside its network
and publicly everywhere else.

14
00:00:56.187 --> 00:00:59.225
It selects which SaaS
front door a user reaches.

15
00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.021
It enforces security policy,

16
00:01:01.021 --> 00:01:04.613
blocking a known-bad domain
before the connection is made.

17
00:01:04.613 --> 00:01:07.316
It carries certificate validation,

18
00:01:07.316 --> 00:01:12.363
because OCSP responders and CRL
distribution points are DNS names.

19
00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:17.263
It's the stable service identity that
Zero Trust policy is written against.

20
00:01:17.263 --> 00:01:21.213
And a wildcard declares a whole
namespace of applications.

21
00:01:21.213 --> 00:01:24.905
NIST's 2026 deployment
guide says DNS has become

22
00:01:24.905 --> 00:01:28.137
more than a service;
it's a security control.

23
00:01:28.137 --> 00:01:32.938
Yet most agencies run it on as many as
five platforms that share no state.

24
00:01:32.938 --> 00:01:36.156
The answer is one
authoritative DDI policy plane,

25
00:01:36.156 --> 00:01:38.880
a requirement rather than a product.

26
00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.370
Run those platforms independently
and you get split-brain DNS:

27
00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:48.118
the same name returning different answers
depending on where the query starts.

28
00:01:48.118 --> 00:01:50.229
Designed split-horizon is fine.

29
00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:54.967
The problem is unintentional drift
between systems with no shared state.

30
00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:56.604
It takes three forms.

31
00:01:56.604 --> 00:02:00.697
Private-IP split-brain: an EC2
instance gets an Azure private

32
00:02:00.697 --> 00:02:05.280
endpoint's private address only
through a Route 53 forwarding rule.

33
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.668
If the rule is missing or stale, the name
still resolves, to the public address.

34
00:02:10.668 --> 00:02:13.634
Microsoft attributes roughly 70 percent of

35
00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:17.242
private-endpoint access-denied
cases to exactly that.

36
00:02:17.242 --> 00:02:22.295
SaaS split-brain: GCC uses the
Worldwide endpoint list, so the risk is

37
00:02:22.295 --> 00:02:27.618
path consistency, a stale forwarder
or browser DoH to a public resolver.

38
00:02:27.618 --> 00:02:32.829
And security-policy split-brain: a block
applies only on the resolver that answers,

39
00:02:32.829 --> 00:02:36.430
so the posture is the weakest
resolver a client can reach.

40
00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.822
The failure is silent, the change
rate outruns any manual process,

41
00:02:40.822 --> 00:02:43.180
and every system has its own timing.

42
00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:47.630
Only a control plane that holds the
policy objects keeps them in step.

43
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.798
The federal mandates never name a product,

44
00:02:50.798 --> 00:02:53.630
but together they describe
a governed name layer.

45
00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.380
It's worth being precise
about who requires what.

46
00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:02.156
OMB M-22-09 requires encrypted DNS
wherever it's technically supported,

47
00:03:02.156 --> 00:03:07.355
with endpoints explicitly configured
to use agency-designated resolvers.

48
00:03:07.355 --> 00:03:12.755
It also notes that DNS must already be
routed through CISA-operated infrastructure.

49
00:03:12.755 --> 00:03:17.275
The requirement that all egress
resolution go through Protective

50
00:03:17.275 --> 00:03:21.230
DNS comes from CISA's own
guidance, which cites the statute.

51
00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:26.536
OMB M-26-14, which rescinded M-21-31
in May 2026, doesn't name DNS,

52
00:03:26.536 --> 00:03:33.358
but it requires logs that show source
and destination addresses, protocols,

53
00:03:33.358 --> 00:03:39.855
ports and sessions, searchable for
six months and retrievable for a year.

54
00:03:39.855 --> 00:03:46.193
M-08-23 and SP 800-53 carry DNSSEC.

55
00:03:46.193 --> 00:03:52.571
And version 3.3 of the TIC 3.0
catalog, published in September 2026,

56
00:03:52.571 --> 00:03:57.854
lists five DNS capabilities, from
sinkholing to Protective DNS.

57
00:03:57.854 --> 00:04:02.873
NIST SP 800-81 Revision 3,
final on March 19, 2026,

58
00:04:02.873 --> 00:04:09.343
is the practical guide to all of
this: guidance, not a mandate.

59
00:04:09.343 --> 00:04:13.310
And FedRAMP certifies a cloud
service, not an architecture;

60
00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.830
the agency still authorizes
its own DDI system.

61
00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.080
What has to change starts
with Active Directory.

62
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.997
For twenty years AD has been the hidden
DNS service of the on-premises network:

63
00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:28.915
a domain's name becomes a zone,
and every domain-joined machine

64
00:04:28.915 --> 00:04:32.680
points at the domain controllers
for all of its resolution.

65
00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:37.483
Netlogon on each domain controller
registers the SRV locator records

66
00:04:37.483 --> 00:04:41.717
that clients use to find LDAP,
Kerberos and the global catalog.

67
00:04:41.717 --> 00:04:44.843
Secure dynamic update keeps
those records current.

68
00:04:44.843 --> 00:04:49.792
Forwarders, GlobalNames, reverse and
signed zones each need a decision.

69
00:04:49.792 --> 00:04:52.968
Microsoft Entra ID inherits none of it.

70
00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:56.324
It hosts no zones and
publishes no locator records,

71
00:04:56.324 --> 00:05:00.400
yet hybrid-joined devices still
look for a domain controller,

72
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.156
and Entra Domain Services adds
a DNS authority of its own.

73
00:05:04.156 --> 00:05:10.076
So the locator records stay where domain
controllers can register them securely,

74
00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:14.082
on AD DNS or a DDI platform
that accepts GSS-TSIG updates,

75
00:05:14.082 --> 00:05:16.868
until the last domain controller goes.

76
00:05:16.868 --> 00:05:20.970
Everything else moves zone by
zone, once it's inventoried.

77
00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.757
Every control in the policy plane
assumes the query reaches it.

78
00:05:25.757 --> 00:05:28.859
Three ordinary mechanisms
decide whether it does,

79
00:05:28.859 --> 00:05:31.221
and none of them reports when it doesn't.

80
00:05:31.221 --> 00:05:32.607
First, the edge.

81
00:05:32.607 --> 00:05:37.839
Cisco Catalyst SD-WAN's redirect-dns
action, as one example,

82
00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:43.833
sends branch DNS to a policy-designated
resolver, IPv4 and UDP only.

83
00:05:43.833 --> 00:05:47.127
It must be a network-addressable
agency resolver,

84
00:05:47.127 --> 00:05:52.108
not a loopback agent on the device, and
any SASE resolver belongs behind it.

85
00:05:52.108 --> 00:05:54.330
Second, encrypted DNS.

86
00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.527
DNS over TLS and DNS
over QUIC use port 853,

87
00:05:58.527 --> 00:06:02.971
so block them except
to approved resolvers.

88
00:06:02.971 --> 00:06:07.075
DNS over HTTPS shares port
443 with all web traffic,

89
00:06:07.075 --> 00:06:11.179
so it's governed at the
endpoint: Windows DoH policy,

90
00:06:11.179 --> 00:06:17.532
the Edge and Chrome DoH mode, and an
NXDOMAIN answer for Firefox's canary domain.

91
00:06:17.532 --> 00:06:19.857
Third, rogue DHCP.

92
00:06:19.857 --> 00:06:24.096
One rogue server handing out its
own resolver in option 6 moves a

93
00:06:24.096 --> 00:06:28.733
whole subnet at once, and rogue IPv6
router advertisements do the same.

94
00:06:28.733 --> 00:06:35.220
AD authorization doesn't stop them; DHCP
snooping and RA Guard on the switches do.

95
00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:41.272
A certificate can be valid, unrevoked and
issued for the right name, and still fail,

96
00:06:41.272 --> 00:06:45.940
because every check that makes it
trustworthy starts with a DNS lookup:

97
00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.292
the endpoint, the revocation
endpoints, the FQDN that policy names,

98
00:06:50.292 --> 00:06:53.220
and the sign-in endpoint
for the token's cloud.

99
00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.697
If an agency CRL name
exists only in AD DNS,

100
00:06:56.697 --> 00:07:01.708
a workload in AWS can't resolve
it without a forwarding rule.

101
00:07:01.708 --> 00:07:05.946
The revocation check fails, the
ticket is opened against PKI,

102
00:07:05.946 --> 00:07:08.658
and the root cause can take days to find.

103
00:07:08.658 --> 00:07:13.219
DNSSEC adds origin authentication
and integrity, not encryption.

104
00:07:13.219 --> 00:07:15.825
The program's position follows NIST:

105
00:07:15.825 --> 00:07:19.820
sign external zones and the
dot-gov delegations, always.

106
00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:24.145
Validate on every agency resolver,
with the root trust anchor.

107
00:07:24.145 --> 00:07:28.750
And sign internal zones only where the
system's authorization requires it;

108
00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:33.057
NIST calls internal signing of
limited value for most organizations.

109
00:07:33.057 --> 00:07:37.898
Validation stays on agency resolvers
because Azure DNS and Route 53

110
00:07:37.898 --> 00:07:43.280
sign public zones only, and the default
Azure resolver doesn't validate.

111
00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:48.530
Azure private endpoints are the commonest
source of private-IP split-brain.

112
00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:52.845
When a private endpoint is created,
Azure writes its A record into

113
00:07:52.845 --> 00:07:57.080
a privatelink zone, and public DNS
carries a CNAME into that name.

114
00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:59.505
The private answer
comes back only when the

115
00:07:59.505 --> 00:08:01.930
query reaches a resolver
linked to the zone.

116
00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:06.659
So every origin, on-premises, the
other clouds and the branch path,

117
00:08:06.659 --> 00:08:11.388
forwards the service's public
zone, such as database.windows.net,

118
00:08:11.388 --> 00:08:14.868
to the DNS Private
Resolver's inbound endpoint.

119
00:08:14.868 --> 00:08:16.342
Not the privatelink zone.

120
00:08:16.342 --> 00:08:20.980
Azure Government uses different zone names,
so commercial rules don't carry over.

121
00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:23.968
The Private Resolver
is a recursive bridge,

122
00:08:23.968 --> 00:08:28.118
not an authority: reach it by
forwarding, never by delegation.

123
00:08:28.118 --> 00:08:32.159
Give each endpoint a dedicated
subnet and a static address,

124
00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.293
avoid ruleset loops, and
deploy one per region.

125
00:08:35.293 --> 00:08:37.447
Addresses need the same discipline,

126
00:08:37.447 --> 00:08:41.255
with the enterprise IPAM as the
authority for address blocks.

127
00:08:41.255 --> 00:08:47.121
AWS VPC IPAM can allocate from an external
IPAM in commercial Regions, but not

128
00:08:47.121 --> 00:08:52.990
in GovCloud; on Azure and Google Cloud,
mirror the reservations and reconcile.

129
00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:58.990
A branch client's query passes the SD-WAN
edge to an agency-controlled resolver,

130
00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:03.271
then the DDI policy plane, with
its response policy zones, DNSSEC,

131
00:09:03.271 --> 00:09:07.390
IPAM and forwarding rules, then
the per-cloud private zones.

132
00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:11.603
Internet-bound recursion leaves
through CISA's Protective DNS.

133
00:09:11.603 --> 00:09:15.328
Five decisions carry almost
all of the operational risk.

134
00:09:15.328 --> 00:09:20.489
One: the redirect-dns target is a
network-addressable agency resolver.

135
00:09:20.489 --> 00:09:26.227
Two: the agency resolver is the first hop,
and any other resolver is its forwarder.

136
00:09:26.227 --> 00:09:31.066
Three: the policy plane holds every
per-cloud conditional forwarding rule.

137
00:09:31.066 --> 00:09:37.328
Four: AD DNS is inventoried and reprovisioned
before any domain controller goes.

138
00:09:37.328 --> 00:09:42.278
Five: OCSP and CRL endpoints
resolve from every origin.

139
00:09:42.278 --> 00:09:45.512
Each fails silently: the
query still resolves,

140
00:09:45.512 --> 00:09:48.577
just outside policy or
to the wrong address.

141
00:09:48.577 --> 00:09:52.752
So each comes with a check, run
in the live configuration rather

142
00:09:52.752 --> 00:09:57.130
than the design document, before
the next SD-WAN or DDI change.

143
00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:58.730
Who owns it?

144
00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:03.844
In an AD estate, DNS belonged to
whoever ran the domain controllers;

145
00:10:03.844 --> 00:10:07.843
DnsAdmins has full control
of every zone by default.

146
00:10:07.843 --> 00:10:10.901
A single policy plane
concentrates that authority,

147
00:10:10.901 --> 00:10:14.742
so its owners have to be named on
purpose, one decider per decision.

148
00:10:14.742 --> 00:10:18.049
The sponsor and CIO decide the platform

149
00:10:18.049 --> 00:10:21.756
model and when AD DNS
and Windows DHCP retire.

150
00:10:21.756 --> 00:10:24.812
The CISO and ISSO set the requirements:

151
00:10:24.812 --> 00:10:29.630
designated resolvers, allowed
transports, DNSSEC and logging.

152
00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:35.117
The authorizing official accepts residual
risk, including every standing exception.

153
00:10:35.117 --> 00:10:40.685
The DNS/DDI team runs the policy plane
and decides what the edge points at;

154
00:10:40.685 --> 00:10:43.468
network engineering configures it.

155
00:10:43.468 --> 00:10:48.030
AD administrators own the
inventory until AD DNS retires,

156
00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:52.306
and cloud teams run their DNS
as consumers of the plane.

157
00:10:52.306 --> 00:10:54.667
Record changes come in three classes.

158
00:10:54.667 --> 00:10:58.755
Standard changes inside a delegated
namespace are pre-approved.

159
00:10:58.755 --> 00:11:02.233
Policy-driven records, like
private endpoint entries,

160
00:11:02.233 --> 00:11:06.792
need no per-change approval, but the
policy itself is change-controlled.

161
00:11:06.792 --> 00:11:11.092
High-impact changes, zones,
delegations, forwarding, SRV,

162
00:11:11.092 --> 00:11:15.550
wildcards and response policy,
go to the change board.

163
00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:17.325
How do you get there?

164
00:11:17.325 --> 00:11:23.789
In six phases: inventory, coexist,
migrate authority, federate the clouds,

165
00:11:23.789 --> 00:11:28.637
decommission AD DNS and DHCP,
then harden and evidence.

166
00:11:28.637 --> 00:11:31.333
Each phase ends with
a rollback point and a

167
00:11:31.333 --> 00:11:34.799
resolution test from every
origin, not a calendar date.

168
00:11:34.799 --> 00:11:38.772
Every move of authority,
whether a zone, a forwarder,

169
00:11:38.772 --> 00:11:42.650
a DHCP scope or a wave of
clients, runs the same loop.

170
00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:47.200
Lower the TTL to 300 seconds
at least 48 hours ahead.

171
00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:53.963
Import with a dry run, never moving
the SOA, apex NS or AD locator records.

172
00:11:53.963 --> 00:11:56.024
Validate from every origin.

173
00:11:56.024 --> 00:12:00.600
Switch, then hold and observe, while
the old authority keeps answering.

174
00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:05.369
In Azure Private DNS, removing a
virtual network link deletes the records

175
00:12:05.369 --> 00:12:09.825
autoregistered through it, and
Microsoft says they can't be restored.

176
00:12:09.825 --> 00:12:13.862
So unlinking is safe only for
zones without autoregistration.

177
00:12:13.862 --> 00:12:18.743
Decommissioning is gated, never
scheduled: the inventory reconciles,

178
00:12:18.743 --> 00:12:24.297
every critical SRV record resolves for
30 consecutive days, 90 days of parallel

179
00:12:24.297 --> 00:12:29.630
operation pass cleanly, failover is
tested, and the old servers are idle.

180
00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:33.543
Once it's running, one policy
plane is only an improvement

181
00:12:33.543 --> 00:12:36.705
if divisions can still run
their own networks through it.

182
00:12:36.705 --> 00:12:42.022
So each division gets a scope covering
its own zones, IP spaces and DHCP scopes,

183
00:12:42.022 --> 00:12:44.846
with manager, operator and auditor roles,

184
00:12:44.846 --> 00:12:48.418
and automation accounts are
held to the same scopes.

185
00:12:48.418 --> 00:12:52.945
Infrastructure as code, ITSM
workflows and ACME DNS-01

186
00:12:52.945 --> 00:12:58.479
certificate automation all go
through one API and one audit trail.

187
00:12:58.479 --> 00:13:02.368
Locator records are resolved
every 15 minutes from every site

188
00:13:02.368 --> 00:13:06.255
and virtual network until the
last domain controller retires.

189
00:13:06.255 --> 00:13:09.999
Drift reports compare the source
of truth with every copy,

190
00:13:09.999 --> 00:13:13.742
and hygiene jobs catch stale
records and dangling CNAMEs.

191
00:13:13.742 --> 00:13:17.531
The evidence floor is M-26-14.

192
00:13:17.531 --> 00:13:22.171
It doesn't name DNS, but it requires
logs that show source and destination

193
00:13:22.171 --> 00:13:27.507
addresses, ports and sessions, searchable
for six months and retrievable for a year,

194
00:13:27.507 --> 00:13:32.380
and readily available to the agency's
top-level security operations center.

195
00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:35.137
Query logs, encrypted queries included,

196
00:13:35.137 --> 00:13:40.568
and DHCP lease history with MAC and IP
addresses are how the name layer meets it.

197
00:13:40.568 --> 00:13:44.500
The lease history is what
turns an address into a device.

198
00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:49.163
Choose the platform on the evidence
it produces for your own boundary.

199
00:13:49.163 --> 00:13:52.443
Commercial DDI licensing
is quoted per agency,

200
00:13:52.443 --> 00:13:55.387
so get every quote on
the same assumptions.

201
00:13:55.387 --> 00:14:00.063
The cloud-native pieces have public
meters, and appear in every model.

202
00:14:00.063 --> 00:14:05.815
Azure DNS Private Resolver charges per
endpoint: 180 dollars a month for each

203
00:14:05.815 --> 00:14:11.388
inbound endpoint and each outbound
endpoint, plus 2 dollars 50 per ruleset.

204
00:14:11.388 --> 00:14:16.044
A hub with one of each is about
362 dollars 50 a month per region,

205
00:14:16.044 --> 00:14:18.326
and a second region doubles it.

206
00:14:18.326 --> 00:14:21.613
Authorization belongs to
an offering, not a vendor.

207
00:14:21.613 --> 00:14:25.318
As one example, Infoblox
Government Cloud has been

208
00:14:25.318 --> 00:14:29.288
FedRAMP Certified at Moderate
since December 15, 2022.

209
00:14:29.288 --> 00:14:32.784
BlueCat and EfficientIP have
no Marketplace listing,

210
00:14:32.784 --> 00:14:35.349
so they run inside the agency's boundary.

211
00:14:35.349 --> 00:14:39.700
And a Moderate authorization
doesn't cover GCC High or DoD.

212
00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:41.113
Some risk remains.

213
00:14:41.113 --> 00:14:44.225
Sync lag becomes the new
source of divergence.

214
00:14:44.225 --> 00:14:46.737
A management-plane outage blocks changes,

215
00:14:46.737 --> 00:14:49.537
though resolution
continues from local state.

216
00:14:49.537 --> 00:14:55.680
And encrypted DNS bypass stays possible
wherever endpoint controls are incomplete.

217
00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:57.805
So, four decisions.

218
00:14:57.805 --> 00:15:02.287
Authorize a single authoritative DDI
policy plane, and assign an owner,

219
00:15:02.287 --> 00:15:06.617
typically the enterprise architecture
or network operations lead.

220
00:15:06.617 --> 00:15:10.826
Have that owner settle the five
configuration decisions within 90 days,

221
00:15:10.826 --> 00:15:14.193
and report the residual risk
to the authorizing official.

222
00:15:14.193 --> 00:15:16.630
Choose the platform model on evidence:

223
00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:21.429
authorization at the right impact level,
and quotes on the same assumptions.

224
00:15:21.429 --> 00:15:24.654
And name the owners, one decider per row,

225
00:15:24.654 --> 00:15:29.893
with the DNS/DDI team deciding
what every branch edge points at.

226
00:15:29.893 --> 00:15:35.561
The next step is Phase 1, the inventory:
export every AD DNS zone, SRV record,

227
00:15:35.561 --> 00:15:40.946
conditional forwarder and DHCP
scope, and every redirect-dns target

228
00:15:40.946 --> 00:15:46.331
and revocation endpoint, before
anyone touches a domain controller.

229
00:15:46.331 --> 00:15:50.805
It's read-only, and it's the one step
whose omission can't be repaired later.

230
00:15:50.805 --> 00:15:53.137
Govern the name layer as one plane,

231
00:15:53.137 --> 00:15:57.217
and it can carry the cloud,
branch and identity transitions.

232
00:15:57.217 --> 00:16:01.292
Book 08 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

233
00:16:01.292 --> 00:16:02.120
Thank you.
